+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This review covers the Spring Boot accounts API, its multi-tenant authorization boundary, H2/JPA persistence, payment
+dispatch and queue, closure and case workflows, and the configured scheme, fraud, ledger, and notification integrations.
+It is based on the implementation and configuration in this repository, not on production telemetry or deployment
+manifests. “Detection” is the ability to detect the failure before customer or downstream impact: 1 means almost
+certain/preventive detection and 10 means unlikely to be detected without a customer or reconciliation report.
+
+Scores use the conventional FMEA direction: severity (S) and occurrence (O) are 1–10, and detection (D) is 1–10, with
+higher values worse. RPN = S × O × D. Severity considers customer, financial, regulatory, confidentiality, and
+downstream operational impact. Occurrence is a qualitative estimate from the code and architecture because no incident
+history is available. Prioritize RPNs of 150 or more, and any S of 9 or 10 even when the RPN is lower.
+
+| S | Impact guide | O | Likelihood guide | D | Detection guide |
+|---:|---|---:|---|---:|---|
+| 1–3 | Local inconvenience, quickly recoverable | 1–3 | Rare or requires unusual conditions | 1–3 | Automated control/alert normally catches it |
+| 4–6 | Material delay or support/manual recovery | 4–6 | Plausible during normal operation | 4–6 | Logs/metrics or reconciliation usually needed |
+| 7–8 | Significant financial, service, or data impact | 7–8 | Likely under common faults/load | 7–8 | Often found by customers or downstream teams |
+| 9–10 | Major financial loss, security breach, or regulatory impact | 9–10 | Expected without a control | 9–10 | No reliable built-in detection |
+
+## Failure modes
+
+| ID | Failure mode and cause | Effect on customers and dependent systems/teams | Current detection | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| FMEA-01 | **Data loss on restart or instance replacement.** `application.yml` uses `jdbc:h2:mem:accounts;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1`, with `create-drop` schema generation and fixture initialization. | Accounts, balances, transactions, cases, notes, closure requests, and payment state disappear when the process/JVM ends. Customers can see missing accounts or stale/incorrect balances; support, reconciliation, ledger, and compliance teams lose the operational record. | No persistence-loss alarm or startup data-integrity check is implemented; likely discovered by users or reconciliation. | 10 | 6 | 9 | **540** | Use the production durable database configuration and migrations; remove fixture loading from production; add backup/restore tests, startup schema checks, and a data-loss/runbook alert. |
+| FMEA-02 | **Duplicate or inconsistent payment after timeout/retry.** Scheme submission is an external side effect, while the local status is saved only afterward; a timeout can leave the scheme submitted but the service unaware. A client or worker retry can submit again. | Payee may receive duplicate payments, or the customer may see an ambiguous/failed payment. Scheme, ledger, support, and finance teams must investigate duplicates and reverse entries. | Scheme duplicate responses may reject some repeats, but there is no durable idempotency/reconciliation workflow or alert for ambiguous submissions. | 9 | 6 | 8 | **432** | Make the payment ID/idempotency key durable and enforce uniqueness end-to-end at the scheme; use an outbox/state machine, query status after timeout, and reconcile scheme instructions against local payments. |
+| FMEA-03 | **Scheme accepted but ledger posting fails or times out.** The dispatcher marks `POSTING_FAILED`, but the account is debited locally before the ledger call and there is no retry or compensating action. | Customer’s displayed balance is reduced while the core ledger may not be debited; balances diverge and payment finality is unclear. Finance and ledger operations need manual repair. | A warning log and `POSTING_FAILED` state exist, but no metric, alert, retry, or automated reconciliation is present. | 9 | 5 | 7 | **315** | Use an idempotent ledger reference, durable retry/outbox, explicit pending-reconciliation state, and alert on aged `POSTING_FAILED`; define compensation and ledger reconciliation procedures. |
+| FMEA-04 | **Concurrent payments bypass balance or daily-limit checks.** `paidToday` and `account.debit` are not protected by an account lock/version check; concurrent requests can read the same balance and limit. | Overspend, negative local balance, or daily-limit breach; scheme and ledger may receive payments the account should have rejected. Customers, fraud, finance, and support teams are affected. | No invariant check, optimistic-lock conflict, or alert exists; likely detected by reconciliation or customer complaint. | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Add optimistic/pessimistic account locking or an atomic reservation/debit operation; enforce non-negative balance and daily-limit invariants in the database; load-test concurrent payment requests. |
+| FMEA-05 | **Queued payment is never dispatched.** The only worker runs at 06:00 UK time on weekdays; there is no persistent job ownership, retry/backoff, or catch-up monitoring. A crashed instance, disabled scheduler, or deployment during the run leaves `QUEUED` records behind. | Payments execute late or not at all; customers miss bill/payment timing, while scheme, support, and operations teams receive avoidable queries. | The queue is queryable only through the payment API; no age/lag metric, job heartbeat, or alert is implemented. | 8 | 6 | 8 | **384** | Emit job start/end, count, duration, failures, and queue age metrics; alert on missed runs and aged queued items; use a durable worker/claim-and-retry mechanism and an operator replay tool. |
+| FMEA-06 | **Worker stops part-way through a batch.** `runDue` loops without per-payment isolation or claim state; an exception can abort the transaction and leave processing dependent on the next run. | Some customers’ payments are delayed; repeated execution can also create duplicate external instructions if the scheme call succeeded before failure. Operations cannot tell which items are safe to replay. | Only application failure logs, if any; no per-payment attempt, lease, or batch progress record. | 8 | 5 | 8 | **320** | Process each payment with durable attempt/status transitions and idempotency, isolate failures, record a dead-letter/retry state, and expose batch progress and replay controls. |
+| FMEA-07 | **Fraud screening is not part of the payment path.** Fraud client and configuration exist, but no production code invokes `FraudScreeningClient` before dispatch. | Payments can bypass the bank’s intended fraud decisioning, increasing unauthorized-loss, AML/fraud, and regulatory exposure. Fraud operations may have no event to investigate. | No metric or audit event proves that screening occurred; absence is only visible by code review or downstream fraud losses. | 10 | 7 | 10 | **700** | Integrate screening as an explicit fail-closed payment state before scheme submission; persist decision and correlation ID, handle unavailable/REFER outcomes, alert on bypasses, and add contract tests proving every payment is screened. |
+| FMEA-08 | **Scheme integration returns an unexpected error or response.** The client handles only HTTP 4xx as business rejection and resource-access timeouts; 5xx, malformed bodies, and other runtime failures escape as generic errors. | Payment creation may return 5xx after an uncertain external outcome, with no consistent payment status or customer-facing explanation. API consumers retry and support handles ambiguous cases. | HTTP error logs/tracebacks may exist, but there are no integration metrics, bounded retries, circuit breaker, or reconciliation alert. | 8 | 5 | 7 | **280** | Classify all response classes and malformed payloads; add correlation IDs, bounded retry only for safe/idempotent calls, circuit breaking, explicit `UNKNOWN`/reconciliation state, and alerts by error class. |
+| FMEA-09 | **Notification failure is hidden.** `notifySent` catches all runtime exceptions and only logs a warning; the notification client has no configured timeout or durable delivery. | Customer does not receive payment confirmation, creating uncertainty and duplicate support contacts; notification operations cannot reliably measure loss. Payment itself may be completed, so naïve retrying is unsafe. | Warning log only; no delivery status, queue, metric, or alert. | 5 | 6 | 8 | **240** | Publish notifications through a durable outbox/queue after payment state commit; add provider timeout, delivery status, retry/dead-letter handling, and alert on notification lag/failure rate. |
+| FMEA-10 | **Tenant or role claim is wrong/stale, or authorization logic regresses.** Resource access depends on JWT `tenant`, `roles`, and `sub` claims plus distributed `AccessPolicy` checks. | A customer/staff member may receive another tenant’s account or staff-only data, or legitimate users may be denied. This can cause a serious confidentiality breach and regulatory incident; identity, security, support, and tenant teams are affected. | Signature validation and API tests cover representative cases, but there is no authorization decision audit, tenant-boundary canary, or alert for cross-tenant access attempts. | 10 | 3 | 8 | **240** | Bind issuer/audience/key rotation explicitly; centralize and audit authorization decisions; add property-based tenant-isolation tests, deny-by-default integration tests for every endpoint, and alert on anomalous 403/404/access patterns. |
+| FMEA-11 | **Write operations lack the documented scope control.** Case creation and closure-request creation are authenticated and role/resource checked, but do not require `accounts:write`, despite README’s convention that data changes need it. | A valid customer token with an otherwise broad/unintended role/scope can create cases or closure requests. This can create operational noise or initiate account lifecycle actions; IAM and support/approver teams are affected. | Existing tests check role/resource behavior, not missing write scope; no policy-drift scan or authorization audit exists. | 7 | 5 | 8 | **280** | Add `accounts:write` (or endpoint-specific write scopes) to every mutating endpoint; add negative tests with read-only tokens and an automated endpoint/policy inventory check. |
+| FMEA-12 | **Closure request race or incomplete workflow.** Duplicate prevention is a read-then-write `existsBy...` check without a database uniqueness constraint; this repository exposes request/list endpoints but no approval mutation that closes the account. | Concurrent requests can create multiple pending closures; or a pending/approved request remains unresolved and the account never closes. Customers receive inconsistent lifecycle status, while approvers and support teams must reconcile manually. | Conflicts are returned for sequential duplicates; no uniqueness constraint, SLA/age alert, or end-to-end closure workflow monitor exists. | 7 | 5 | 8 | **280** | Add a database uniqueness constraint for one pending request per account, make approval/rejection/close a transactional workflow with authorization and audit trail, and alert on aged pending requests. |
+| FMEA-13 | **Sensitive payment data is overexposed in API responses/logging.** `PaymentView` returns full payee account and sort-code data; integration failures can log exception details, and there is no structured redaction policy. | Compromise or accidental access exposes payment destination data; customers, staff, security, and compliance teams face privacy and fraud impact. | Security tests cover endpoint access but not field minimization or log-redaction scanning. | 8 | 4 | 8 | **256** | Return only the minimum masked destination fields; prohibit sensitive values in logs/traces; add redaction tests and centralized structured logging with access audit events. |
+| FMEA-14 | **Read path overload or database failure.** Account and transaction reads use database queries directly, with no pagination, caching, pool/timeouts, or resilience controls; transaction lists can grow without bound. | API latency and 5xx responses interrupt customer and support access; dependent channels and operations queues back up. | No application metrics, database health alert, query-latency SLO, or endpoint rate limit is configured; health permit is present but no actuator dependency is declared. | 7 | 6 | 6 | **252** | Add production database connection/statement timeouts, indexes and pagination, request limits, pool metrics, endpoint latency/error SLOs, and a real readiness/liveness probe with dependency-appropriate health checks. |
+| FMEA-15 | **Time boundary and audit timestamps disagree.** Payment scheduling uses an injected UK clock, but `ClosureRequest` and `SupportCase` use `Instant.now()` directly; cutoff/working-day behavior also depends on local UK time and daylight-saving transitions. | Payments may execute a day late/early around cutoff or holidays, and case/closure audit ordering can be inconsistent. Customers, support, scheme operations, and compliance reviewers see confusing timelines. | Unit tests use a mutable clock for payment behavior, but direct `Instant.now()` paths and DST/holiday boundary tests are not covered. | 6 | 4 | 7 | **168** | Inject the clock into every domain workflow, define business-calendar and cutoff policy centrally, test DST/holiday/cutoff boundaries, and persist/display an explicit event timezone where relevant. |
+| FMEA-16 | **Unhandled domain/database/runtime exception produces an opaque 500.** `ApiExceptionHandler` maps known errors and validation only; integration 5xxs, data corruption (`Enum.valueOf`), and persistence errors have no stable error contract or correlation ID. | Customers and API consumers receive inconsistent failures and may retry unsafe operations; support lacks a traceable incident context. | Server logs may show a stack trace, but no standardized 5xx metric, correlation ID, or error-budget alert is implemented. | 6 | 6 | 6 | **216** | Add a last-resort redacted 5xx handler with correlation ID, structured error metrics, safe retry guidance, and alerting; ensure transaction rollback and idempotency behavior are tested for every external failure. |
+
+## Highest-priority release actions
+
+Before release, the risk should be reduced in this order:
+
+1. Replace the in-memory/create-drop persistence path with the approved durable database, migrations, backup/restore, and
+   data-integrity monitoring (FMEA-01).
+2. Close the payment consistency loop: scheme and ledger idempotency, timeout reconciliation, concurrency protection,
+   durable queue/retry processing, and alerts for `POSTING_FAILED`/aged `QUEUED` payments (FMEA-02 through FMEA-06).
+3. Put fraud screening in the payment path and fail closed when it is unavailable or returns REFER/REJECT (FMEA-07).
+4. Correct write-scope enforcement and add negative authorization tests, then add tenant-isolation and audit monitoring
+   (FMEA-10 and FMEA-11).
+5. Add operational instrumentation: request/integration metrics, scheduler heartbeat and queue age, correlation IDs,
+   notification delivery state, 5xx handling, and reconciliation dashboards (FMEA-08, FMEA-09, FMEA-14, FMEA-16).
+
+## Residual-risk validation
+
+The FMEA should be revisited with production-like evidence after the first release candidate: payment duplicate and ledger
+reconciliation results, queue age and scheduler success, scheme/fraud/ledger/notification error rates, authorization-denial
+and cross-tenant test results, database restore time, and the rate of customer-reported missing or ambiguous payments.
